s6-2-p16 = prose (cons "When the residual encounters a code component that is not static, it may retain a call to the represented evaluator. That call is still an ordinary program term and is natively realized by §9.2. The distinction between static and dynamic code belongs to the chosen specialization, not to the set of programs that the language can express. Raw syntax can be evaluated by a guarded process without asserting normalization of arbitrary raw programs; the typed quotation and self-application equations above still require their stated stage indices and available checked code. Guarded representation of divergence does not make an arbitrary diverging term converge. Self-reference also has established limits: Church and Turing’s undecidability results and the diagonal constructions of Lawvere, Gödel, and Tarski concern the precise representability and logical premises involved ([1](#ref-1), [2](#ref-2), [33](#ref-33), [34](#ref-34), [35](#ref-35)); none is bypassed by reifying a program as data. Concretely, an inductive raw-code datatype \(\mathsf{Raw}\) can be consumed by a guarded interpreter \(\mathsf{runRaw}:\mathsf{Raw}\to\mathsf{Env}\to\mathsf{Proc}_{\Sigma}(\mathsf{Value}+\mathsf{Fault})\); its self-application to a code presentation of \(\mathsf{runRaw}\) is a *guarded execution*, not an assertion of a total normalizer or a universe-valued truth predicate. This requires the interpreter's code to be representable as raw syntax and its recursive calls to satisfy the guardedness rule. In contrast, \(q_{A_0}(e)\), \(q_{A_1}(s)\), and \(\mathsf{lift}_A\) in (6.F2)–(6.F4) inhabit checked, explicitly staged code types. No map quoting an arbitrary semantic value at its own level follows from the raw interpreter." nil)
